+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Twelve shared tablets capture plot visits offline for up to 48 hours. Workers compare field notes and sometimes correct the same visit independently, so two edits derived from the same starting state can reach the server days apart. Arrival order carries no information about which observations are more authoritative.
+
+The record store has to define what a visit's current state means under those conditions. The answer determines what a submission must carry, what review work looks like, and which visits can enter the partner's Friday CSV. Tablet-sync and review-export are both sized around it, so reversing it later means reworking local storage on the tablets and the coordinators' review interface together.
+
+Some concurrent edits touch unrelated things — correcting a misspelled observer name while another worker adds a missed species. Others are scientifically coupled — revising a species identification and the cover estimate that goes with it, where accepting one without the other produces a record no observer would stand behind. Field names do not distinguish the two cases.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gate: the server does not judge whether two edits are scientifically related
+
+Inherited from the project's non-goal of automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent. Combining two edits into one record without review asserts that they are independent observations. Nothing in a visit's field structure supports that assertion: a species identification and its cover estimate are separate fields, and an observer list change is sometimes a correction to who made an identification.
+
+### Criterion: preservation of whole-visit scientific intent — major
+
+A submitted visit is one observer's account of one plot on one date. A current record assembled from parts of two accounts may be internally inconsistent in ways neither submitter would accept. Turns on the tenets *do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*.
+
+### Criterion: operable by two coordinators without a systems administrator — major
+
+The team has two coordinators and no dedicated systems staff. Anything requiring ongoing custodianship — rules tuned as the species list and field set evolve — has no owner. Turns on the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+
+### Criterion: coordinator review workload — moderate
+
+Roughly 6,000 visits a season across 24 workers. Review work that could have been avoided costs coordinator time every week, and the Friday export is a recurring deadline.
+
+### Criterion: export readiness — moderate
+
+Visits held out of the export reach the science partner a week late. Goal: make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export, with the partner's columns exported without spreadsheet cleanup.
+
+### Criterion: resistance to careless resolution under queue pressure — moderate
+
+A review queue mostly full of trivial differences trains coordinators to clear items without reading them, which costs more than the review work saves — the two scientifically coupled edits that needed attention are cleared with the same reflex as fifty observer-name corrections.
+
+### Criterion: implementation burden — moderate
+
+First release, small team.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** A visit is an ordered chain of complete revisions. Every submission names the revision it was derived from. A submission whose base is the current head extends the chain and becomes the new head. A submission whose base is an earlier revision is stored as a competing revision, and the visit holds unresolved work until a coordinator resolves it. Resolution names which content becomes the new head and records the coordinator and the contributing submissions. Original submissions are never mutated or deleted; the chain accumulates.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A fetches revision 3 and corrects an observer name. Worker B fetches revision 3 and adds a missing species. A's edit arrives first and becomes revision 4. B's edit names base revision 3, so it is stored as a competing revision and the visit becomes unresolved — even though the two edits touch different fields. A coordinator sees both complete visits side by side and produces revision 5.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Gate: **passes**. The server classifies submissions by base revision only; relatedness of content is never evaluated.
+- Preservation of whole-visit scientific intent: **strong**. Every revision is a complete visit that one author submitted or one coordinator composed and signed. No record is ever assembled by machinery.
+- Operable without a systems administrator: **strong**. The server's behavior is a base-revision comparison. There are no rules to maintain as the species list or field set changes.
+- Coordinator review workload: **weak**. Edits to disjoint fields generate review work identical to edits that genuinely conflict. Two workers correcting one visit from the same base always costs a coordinator.
+- Export readiness: **partial**. Unresolved visits are held out of the export set until a coordinator acts, so throughput depends on the queue being cleared before Friday.
+- Resistance to careless resolution: **weak**. The queue is dominated by trivial differences, which is exactly the condition that produces rubber-stamping.
+- Implementation burden: **strong**. A revision chain, a base-revision comparison, and one resolution operation.
+
+**Summary.** Buys a guarantee that no record is machine-assembled, and pays for it in review volume.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission carries the base state it started from and the field-level changes made against it. The server performs a three-way merge per field with type-appropriate rules: scalars (observation date, notes) take the single changed value and conflict when both sides changed it; the observer list merges as a set of additions and removals; species cover estimates merge per entry, keyed by taxon, with per-entry cover values treated as scalars. Changes that touch disjoint fields combine automatically into a new current record carrying per-field provenance — which submission each value came from. Changes that overlap on a field raise a field-level conflict for coordinator review, showing just the contested fields rather than two whole visits. Most concurrent-edit pairs touch disjoint fields, so most visits stay export-eligible without coordinator involvement.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Gate: **eliminated** in its automatic form. Combining disjoint field changes without review is precisely the assertion that the two edits are scientifically unrelated. The variant that routes every combined record through coordinator approval does pass the gate — but then its review volume is the same as whole-visit revisions, and only the presentation improves, so the advantage it is chosen for is gone.
+- Preservation of whole-visit scientific intent: **weak**. A merged record is a visit no observer submitted. A revised identification merged with the prior cover estimate for that taxon yields a current record that is wrong in a way neither original was.
+- Operable without a systems administrator: **weak**. The merge rules are a maintained artifact. Adding a field, changing how species entries are keyed, or handling a taxon split each require a rule decision, and a wrong rule combines records silently.
+- Coordinator review workload: **strong**. Only genuine field overlaps reach the queue.
+- Export readiness: **strong**. Visits with unrelated concurrent edits stay export-eligible with no coordinator action.
+- Resistance to careless resolution: **strong**. Every queue item is a real contest over one field, presented as that field.
+- Implementation burden: **weak**. Per-type merge rules, structured change payloads, base snapshots retained on the tablets, and field-level provenance through to the export.
+
+**Summary.** Buys review volume and export throughput, and pays for it with machinery that can combine scientifically coupled edits without anyone noticing.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The gate removes automatic merging from contention: the project declines to infer scientific intent, and automatic field combination is that inference wherever two edits are coupled through meaning rather than through a shared field name. What remains of the merge option after the gate — merge with mandatory approval of every combined record — carries the same review volume as whole-visit revisions plus the rule-maintenance burden, so it loses on implementation burden without a compensating gain.
+
+Among the major criteria, whole-visit revisions are strong on both: every current record is a complete visit with a named author or a named coordinator behind it, and the server needs no maintained rules. The option is weak on the two criteria that cut against it — review workload and resistance to careless resolution — and those are moderate, not major. A larger queue of mostly-trivial items is accepted as the cost of the two major guarantees. The decision turns on *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator* and *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*.
+
+**What this forecloses.** Automatic reconciliation of any kind. Two edits to the same visit from the same base always produce coordinator work, however trivial their difference.
+
+**What it obligates downstream.** Tablet-sync retains the base revision ID alongside each local draft and sends it with the submission; keeps its idempotency key across restarts and 48-hour disconnection; refreshes to the new head revision after a successful sync; and shows a worker that a submitted visit is awaiting coordinator resolution rather than treating a competing-revision outcome as a failure to retry. Review-export presents competing revisions as whole visits for comparison, with each one's author and server receipt time; records the resolving coordinator and the contributing submission IDs; holds visits with unresolved work out of the Friday CSV; and surfaces the unresolved queue early enough in the week that it can be cleared before the export.
+
+**What would reopen this.** The accepted cost is a queue two coordinators can clear weekly. If the unresolved queue routinely outlasts the week, the response is to reduce how often two workers edit one visit from the same base — surfacing a visit's pending competing revisions on the tablets before a worker starts editing — rather than to introduce automatic merging. Field-aware merging returns to contention only if the project takes on automated judgments about field relatedness, which the current non-goals exclude.
