 ## Architecture
 
-The record store exposes authenticated submission, current-state retrieval, submission-history retrieval, and review-outcome operations. Persistence keeps original submissions, their authors, and server receipt times. A submission carries an idempotency key so a connection failure does not create another edit on retry.
+The record store exposes authenticated submission, current-state retrieval, revision-history retrieval, unresolved-work listing, and resolution. Persistence keeps original submissions, their authors, and server receipt times.
+
+### Revision Model
 
-A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names.
+- **Revision** — an immutable complete visit state. Carries a revision ID, the visit ID, the plot UUID, the scientific field payload, the identity of the worker who submitted it or the coordinator who composed it, the server receipt time, and the base revision it was derived from.
+- **Head revision** — the single revision designated current for a visit. Retrieval of a visit returns the head's content.
+- **Base revision** — the revision ID a submission names as its starting state. Absent only on the submission that creates a visit.
+- **Competing revision** — a revision accepted with a base other than the head at the time of acceptance. It does not become the head.
+- **Unresolved visit** — a visit holding one or more competing revisions that no resolution has covered. Its competing revisions together with the head form its **unresolved set**, the revisions a coordinator compares.
+- **Resolving revision** — the revision a coordinator's resolution creates. It becomes the head and names the submissions that contributed to it.
 
-The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
+Revisions accumulate. No revision is mutated or deleted once accepted, including the competing revisions a resolution did not select.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. A plot UUID must resolve in the site catalog, including an archived plot, which remains referenceable for historical records.
+
+A submission carries the visit ID, the plot UUID, the base revision ID, the complete scientific field payload, and an idempotency key, so a connection failure does not create another edit on retry. A retry with the same key and an identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse of a key with a different payload is rejected and leaves the original submission unchanged.
+
+Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolving revision. A resolution is a new revision, never an edit to what a worker submitted.
+
+### Classification on Acceptance
+
+A valid submission is classified by its base revision alone:
+
+- No base revision and an unknown visit ID — creates the visit; the revision becomes the head.
+- Base revision equals the current head, and the visit holds no unresolved work — the revision becomes the new head.
+- Base revision is any other revision of this visit, or the visit holds unresolved work — the revision is recorded as a competing revision and the visit is unresolved.
+- Base revision names a revision of another visit, or no known revision — rejected.
+- No base revision and a known visit ID — rejected.
+
+The head does not move while a visit is unresolved. A submission arriving mid-review joins the unresolved set rather than changing the baseline a coordinator is comparing against.
+
+Content is never compared between revisions. A submission whose payload happens to match the head's still classifies by its base, so it can produce unresolved work with no visible difference to resolve.
+
+### Retrieval
+
+| Operation | Returns |
+|---|---|
+| Get visit | Head revision content and ID, whether unresolved work exists, and the count of competing revisions in the unresolved set |
+| Get visit revisions | Every revision of the visit with its author, server receipt time, base revision, originating submission ID, and role (head, competing, superseded, resolving) |
+| List unresolved visits | Visits holding unresolved work, ordered by the earliest server receipt time among their competing revisions |
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+Retrieval distinguishes a usable current state from unresolved review work: a visit's head content is always readable, and the unresolved indicator says whether that content is subject to pending review.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Resolution
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+A coordinator resolves one visit's unresolved set in a single operation. The outcome is a resolving revision whose content is either the content of one revision in the unresolved set, taken unchanged, or content the coordinator composed while comparing them. Composition is a named person's judgment about one visit, which is what the server declines to do on its own.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+Resolution records the coordinator's identity, the resolution time, and the submission IDs of every revision in the unresolved set, so the resolving revision names what was considered and not only what was kept. The resolving revision becomes the head, the visit holds no unresolved work, and its competing revisions remain stored as superseded.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+A resolution covers the whole unresolved set; there is no partial resolution. A later edit based on the new head is an ordinary submission.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+## Export Eligibility
+
+A visit is export-eligible when it holds no unresolved work, and its exported values come from its head revision. Visits with unresolved work are held out until a coordinator resolves them, and appear in the following export. Review-export implements the export itself and owns the partner's columns.
 
 ## Downstream Contract Work
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+Tablet-sync sizes its local storage and retry state machine around this envelope. It retains the base revision ID alongside each local draft and sends it with the submission; keeps its idempotency key across restarts and 48-hour disconnection; refreshes to the new head revision after a successful sync; and presents a competing-revision outcome to the worker as awaiting coordinator resolution rather than as a failure to retry.
+
+Review-export sizes its coordinator interface and export rules around the unresolved-work representation. It compares the revisions in an unresolved set as whole visits, each with its author and server receipt time; invokes resolution with either a selected revision's content or composed content; and surfaces the unresolved queue early enough in the week to be cleared before the Friday export.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions with coordinator resolution | Field-aware three-way merge; last-write-wins | The server does not judge whether two edits are scientifically related, and the team has no owner for maintained merge rules. See `decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md` |
+| Classification of an accepted submission | By base revision alone | Content-equality fast path that skips review when a payload matches the head | Keeps the server out of comparing observation content; an identical-content pair costs one comparison and is rare |
+| Head while a visit is unresolved | Head does not move; later submissions join the unresolved set | Continue advancing the head for submissions based on it | A coordinator compares a stable set; a late submission cannot change the baseline mid-review |
+| Form of a resolution | Always a new resolving revision, even when one revision's content is taken unchanged | Re-point the head at the selected revision | The head is always attributable to a named act, carrying the coordinator and the submissions considered |
+| Coordinator authority over content | May compose content while comparing, not only select one revision | Selection only | A coordinator seeing two complete visits can keep both corrections instead of dropping one; the composition is signed by a named person |
+| Export eligibility | Exclude visits holding unresolved work | Export the head with a flag column for unresolved visits | The partner's CSV carries reviewed state only |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy — whole-visit revisions, per `decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md`.
+2. ✅ Whether a resolution may discard a competing revision — it cannot; competing revisions are retained as superseded.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether a submission whose content matches the head should auto-resolve rather than produce an empty-looking comparison.
+2. Retention of revision chains across seasons, once the first season's volume is known.
+3. Showing a worker that a visit already holds unresolved work before they begin editing — the lever for reducing queue volume without automatic merging. Cascades to tablet-sync.
